## Universal transport

The fibre-containment calculation is the computational heart of universal pullback.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Why lower classes remain lower</summary>

Start below an accepted source, lift an arbitrary target witness upward by the extension law, use acceptance there, and descend inside the lower target class. Since the original witness was arbitrary, the lower source is accepted.
</details>

## Composition reverses on classes

Two relational stages can be composed first or tested from the final class backward, one stage at a time. Both routes agree.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the quantifier calculation</summary>

The assertion that every composite outcome lies in $C$ says: for every first-stage witness, every second-stage witness lies in $C$. This is exactly the iterated universal pullback.
</details>

## Modular requirements and obstructions

Independent requirements can be transported independently because universal pullback preserves intersections.

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

The fibre matrix makes the raw relation visible, while minimal-complement extraction turns failures into an obstruction basis.

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>From antichains to finite excluded-minor lists</summary>

Excluded minors are minimal failures and therefore pairwise incomparable. In a [well-quasi-order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well-quasi-ordering), every antichain is finite and every failure lies above a minimal failure. Consequently, a minor-closed pullback is characterized by avoiding finitely many excluded minors.
</details>
